== Grammar ==

=== Negation ===

==== Simple negation ====
Simple [[Negation (linguistics)|negation]] in Adzera is achieved by the word ''imaʔ'' 'no'. This word can be used on its own in response to a question, or paired with a negative sentence.{{sfnp|Holzknecht|1986|pages=137–138}} For example:

{{interlinear|indent=3
|'''Imaʔ''' Dzi i- bugin biskit
|'''NEG''' 1SG REAL not.like biscuit
|No, I do not like biscuits.{{sfnp|Holzknecht|1986|pages=137–138}}}}

The Amari dialect of Adzera is specifically noted for its use of ''namu'' for 'no' where all other Adzera dialects would use ''imaʔ.'' however, in Amari both words can be used interchangeably.{{sfnp|Holzknecht|1986|pages=137–138}}

==== Negation of a noun phrase ====
The simple negative forms above can be used in a [[noun phrase]] after the noun to modify it. Such as ''mamaʔ namu '<nowiki/>''No children'. This can also apply to a [[Coordinated clause|coordinated noun phrase]], such as ''iyam da ifab '<nowiki/>''dog and pig' where ''iyam da ifab '''namu''''' would mean that there were no dogs and no pigs.{{sfnp|Holzknecht|1986|page=138}}

==== Negation of a verb phrase ====
Most negation is done through the [[verb phrase]]. For general circumstances, verbal negation is achieved by a verbal [[prefix]] ''anuŋʔ-'' And an optional negation [[Grammatical particle|particle]] ''u'' at the end of the sentence.{{sfnp|Holzknecht|1986|page=138}} For example:

{{interlinear|indent=3
|dzi '''anuŋʔ-''' i- saŋʔ rim -a u sib '''u'''
|1SG '''NEG''' REAL be.enough help PTCP 2SG COMP '''NEG'''
|I am not able to help you.{{sfnp|Holzknecht|1986|page=138}}}}

However, for verbs in the [[Imperative mood|imperative]] or [[hortative]] forms, which take a prefix ''wa-'' or ''na-'' respectively, the negative is achieved by replacing their respective prefixes with a negative form ''ma-'' followed at the end of the sentence by a compulsory particle ''maʔ.''{{sfnp|Holzknecht|1986|pages=140–141}}

{{interlinear|indent=3
|'''ma-''' fan '''maʔ'''
|'''IMP.NEG''' go '''IMP.NEG'''
|Do not go!{{sfnp|Holzknecht|1986|pages=140–141}}}}

===== Coordinated verb negation =====
When two negative verbs or phrases are joined by ''da'' ‘and’ the first verb takes the negative prefix ''anuŋʔ-'', and the negative particle ''u'' comes at the end of the whole sentence.{{sfnp|Holzknecht|1986|page=140}}

{{interlinear|indent=3
|{muŋʔ ugu} da sagat '''anuŋʔ-''' i- ga was da i- is pauʔ '''u'''
|a.long.time.ago TIME woman '''NEG''' REAL eat lime and REAL hit tobacco '''NEG'''
|A long time ago, women neither chewed betel nut nor smoked tobacco.{{sfnp|Holzknecht|1986|page=140}}}}

===== Negation with future tense =====
When negating a sentence in the [[future tense]], the future tense prefix is replaced with the [[Realis mood|realis]] prefix. Any future time marking still remains. There is also a preference toward forming negative sentences in the future tense with an [[auxiliary verb]] ''saŋʔ'' 'be able, be enough' before the main verb of the sentence, suggesting a reluctance toward making negative statements about the future.{{sfnp|Holzknecht|1986|pages=139–140}} For example:

{{interlinear|indent=3
|tataʔ da u '''anuŋʔ-''' '''i-''' saŋʔ fa  -da taun '''u'''
|tomorrow TIME 2SG '''NEG''' '''REAL''' be.enough go PTCP town '''NEG'''
|Tomorrow you will not be able to go to town.{{sfnp|Holzknecht|1986|pages=139–140}}}}

When coordinating two sentences of future tense, the first verb phrase replaces the future prefix with the realis, but all following verb phrases retain their future tense marking.{{sfnp|Holzknecht|1986|pages=139–140}}

